Date: 2026-04-24
Descriptor: intrinsic_drive (ADR 0031)
Purpose:
    First v2 mechanism where the system chooses what to do and when
    to stop, based on an internal value signal. Prior ADRs provided
    capabilities (pattern discovery, axiom discovery, theory
    naming); the caller had to decide which one to invoke, with what
    parameters. Under ADR 0031 the RSet carries a global
    `abstraction_score` and a `drive_step` that tries every
    candidate action on a clone and applies the best-improving one.
    `intrinsic_drive` loops this until nothing helps anymore.

Mechanism version:
    ADR 0031 on top of ADR 0018 (autonomous_pass), ADR 0028
    (discover_axioms_minimal), and ADR 0030 (theories).

====================================================================
METRIC
====================================================================

    score = Σ_pattern max(0, (N − 1) × k)     # reuse savings
          + 2.0 · Σ_theory |members|           # theory richness
          − 0.1 · |meta-R edges|               # overhead tax

- `(N−1)·k` is classical MDL reuse savings: N instances of a k-edge
  pattern need only 1 definition, plus N−1 references to it, vs.
  N·k raw edges.
- The per-axiom reward keeps theory discovery competitive with
  pattern discovery on theory-heavy inputs.
- Overhead tax penalizes accumulating unused meta-R.

The metric is not a "true" MDL computation — it's a direction signal
good enough to choose among candidate actions. It's monotonic in the
things we want (reuse, conjunction, minimality) and additive.

====================================================================
ACTION SPACE
====================================================================

Two action families are currently in `candidate_actions()`:

1. `DiscoverPatterns(size)` — runs `autonomous_pass` with
   `discovery.target_size = size`. Tried at every size in
   `config.pattern_sizes` (default [2, 3, 4]).
2. `DiscoverTheory` — runs `discover_theory` then `name_theory` on
   whatever comes out.

Each step:
    for each action in candidate_actions():
        trial = self.clone()
        trial.apply(action)
        delta = trial.score - self.score
        if delta > best.delta: best = (action, trial, delta)
    if best.delta > epsilon:
        self = best.trial   (take the best improvement)
    else:
        stop

Both actions are idempotent under repeat applications (autonomous
pass would re-encounter existing patterns and not re-name them;
name_theory reuses theory id under member-set equality). That is
why the loop terminates — eventually every candidate's delta is 0.

====================================================================
RESULTS ON FOUR DIFFERENT-SHAPED INPUTS
====================================================================

   input                               | action order                 | final
  -------------------------------------|------------------------------|------
  1. mixed graph (pattern-rich)        | patterns(2) → theory          | 14.7
  2. equivalence (theory-rich)         | theory → patterns(4)          | 14.6
  3. strict poset (both)               | patterns(3) → theory          | 11.0
  4. random sparse (low-abstraction)   | patterns(2) → theory          |  4.3

**The action order reflects the input's character**:

- On the mixed graph, the driver reaches for pattern discovery first
  because size-2 matches yield 6 single-edge reuses — immediate
  Δ=+13. Theory discovery only nets Δ=+1.7 (accidental antisymmetry).
- On equivalence, theory discovery is the big win up front (six
  axioms, Δ=+10.7). Pattern discovery at size 4 comes second.
- On the strict poset, patterns-at-size-3 wins first (the diamond's
  k=3 structural pattern with k participants each gives Δ=+5.7),
  then theory (transitivity + antisymmetry, Δ=+5.3).
- On random input, almost nothing pays off. The driver grabs the
  single-edge pattern reuse (Δ=+2.6, since the graph has several
  single-edge instances) and the accidental antisymmetry theory
  (Δ=+1.7), then halts.

The driver correctly identifies that random has far less structure
to abstract over — final score 4.3 vs 14.7 on a similarly-sized
mixed graph. The score works as a "how much is there to understand
here" signal.

====================================================================
TERMINATION
====================================================================

`adr0031_drive_is_idempotent_after_saturation` is the key test: run
the drive to completion, then run it again, and verify no new steps
are taken and the score is unchanged. Passes on every test case.

In every example above, the driver stopped on its own after 2 steps
because further actions produced Δ ≤ epsilon:
- Re-running `DiscoverPatterns(size)` with an already-named pattern
  at that size yields no new pattern (Δ = 0 or slightly negative
  from overhead tax).
- Re-running `DiscoverTheory` when the theory is already named
  finds the same member set and `name_theory` returns the existing
  id — no new edges, Δ ≈ 0.

So the loop is guaranteed to terminate when no action improves
score above epsilon (default 0), which always happens in finite
steps because the state space is bounded.

====================================================================
COMPARISON WITH PRIOR AUTONOMOUS MECHANISMS
====================================================================

- **`autonomous_pass` (0018)**: a *single* end-to-end discover +
  refine + name cycle at *one fixed target_size*. Caller chose
  the size.
- **`autonomous_sweep` (0021)**: loops autonomous_pass over a
  size list. Caller chose the list and the termination.
- **`intrinsic_drive` (0031)**: loops a broader action space (sizes
  + theory), self-selects the best-improving action each step,
  self-terminates.

The qualitative difference is in *who decides*. Before 0031,
external code picked the mechanism, the size, and the stopping
condition. After 0031, the RSet picks them based on its own score.
This is the first mechanism where v2 satisfies the
"self-driven triggering" entry in the initial capability list.

====================================================================
LIMITS
====================================================================

1. **Metric is hand-tuned.** The weights (2.0 per axiom, 0.1
   overhead) are chosen because they produce reasonable action
   orderings on the test inputs, not derived from information-
   theoretic first principles. A better derivation (e.g., true
   MDL bit cost with priors) would make the score more defensible
   but would not change the qualitative behavior here.

2. **Action space is fixed.** The driver cannot invent new actions
   or new parameter settings — it can only pick from the enumerated
   candidates. Extending to (say) cross-size naming policy choices
   or attach-only re-runs would require enlarging
   `candidate_actions()`.

3. **No exploration.** The driver is purely greedy (best improving
   action wins). No "try a worse action to escape a local optimum"
   step; no randomization across ties. On this workload it doesn't
   matter (actions are additive on score), but on a future workload
   with action interactions it might.

4. **No revision.** The driver never retracts a named pattern or
   theory. If an action turned out bad (it didn't here, but could
   under a changed metric), the driver has no mechanism to undo.
   This is a deliberate choice for ADR 0031; the retract_pattern /
   retract_theory primitives are available if a future ADR wants to
   build a more sophisticated revision loop.

5. **Score is a means, not a goal.** High abstraction_score is not
   "better understanding" in any deep sense — it's "more named
   reusable structure," which is a proxy. The driver does not know
   whether the structures it names are the right ones; it only
   knows they pay off locally.

====================================================================
SANITY
====================================================================

- 155 → 162 tests pass (7 new: zero-score on bare RSet, positive
  score on reuse, drive progresses on structured input, halts on
  unstructured, rejects no-improvement step, produces theory on
  poset, idempotent after saturation).
- `cargo run --example intrinsic_drive` produces the table above.
- No existing example was affected; drive is additive only.

====================================================================
COMMITMENT CHECK
====================================================================

- 1 (only R): all drive state is read from RSet, all writes go
  through existing named-object APIs. No new storage. ✓
- 2 (R binary): same. ✓
- 3 (types are meta-R): drive uses patterns (from 0029) and
  theories (from 0030), which are meta-R. Drive itself adds no
  meta-R. ✓
- 4 (token identity): drive doesn't mint identifiers. ✓
- 5 (structural similarity): score is a derived structural
  function; no external labels. ✓

====================================================================
NEXT (per approved A → C → B → D order)
====================================================================

C (this ADR) complete. Next: B — give each registered axiom its own
intension in meta-R, analogous to what ADR 0029 did for patterns.
Once axioms have intension, the theory objects from ADR 0030 will
carry full structural definition, not just a name list.
